<p>As dairy and poultry production increased, the company established plants in Dickinson, Minot, Hettinger, Garrison, Bismarck, and Miles City, Montana. Its Mandan and Minot plants produced an average of approximately 30,000 pounds of butter each day throughout the year, reaching 60,000 pounds daily during peak summer production. The Dickinson plant pasteurized milk and produced ice cream for local delivery.</p>

<p>The company's several plants employed well over 200 people. Its success depended on dairy farms shipping cream from east of Jamestown to Billings, Montana, and from northern South Dakota to the Canadian border.</p>

<p>A two-story brick addition completed in 1934 tripled the Mandan plant's poultry-handling capacity and added nine employees. The main floor became a truck garage, while refrigeration equipment and a new power-generation system were installed in the basement.</p>

<p>Changes in agriculture became apparent during the 1940s. Poultry and dairy production declined steadily after World War II, and commercial growers replaced the company's Mandan-brand turkey operation. Butter and ice-cream production gradually ended as the company expanded into meat processing.</p>

<p>Operations at the downtown plant ceased on March 11, 1988. The creamery and adjacent buildings were demolished for the parking lot that remains on the site.</p>

